> I love how XSLT is being reinvented every step of the way here.

I did not know about XSLT either. For my part, after looking at it, this is exactly what is needed - including the possibility of having templates within templates - except for the part where I still need javascript to make it work.

It would be great if there was a new element (maybe just new attributes?) such that one could write:

`<new-element data-xml-src="data.xml" data-xsl-src="template.xsl"></new-element>`

And the browser would run automatically something like:

```
function loadXMLDoc(filename){
	xhttp = new XMLHttpRequest();
	xhttp.open("GET", filename, false);
	xhttp.send("");

	return xhttp.responseXML;
}

function displayXML(){
	let elements = document.querySelectorAll("new-element");

	elements.forEach(function(element){
		let xml = loadXMLDoc(element.getAttribute("data-xml-src"));
		let xsl = loadXMLDoc(element.getAttribute("data-xsl-src"));

		xsltProcessor = new XSLTProcessor();
		xsltProcessor.importStylesheet(xsl);
		fragment = xsltProcessor.transformToFragment(xml, document);
		element.parentNode.replaceChild(fragment, element);
	});
}

window.addEventListener('load', displayXML);
```

to replace the element with a document fragment stored in *template.xsl*, filled with data found in *data.xml*.

Even better, the template can be included in the XML file (with `<?xml-stylesheet type = "text/xsl" href = "template.xsl"?>`), thus only the attribute `data-xml-src` would be needed. Although the interface `XSLTProcessor` doesn't seem to be able to identify the element `xml-stylesheet`, even though browsers usually recognize it when reading an XML file.

There could even be an attribute available for lazy loading.

 Once you define the appropriate caching protocol server-side (never cache data, only templates), this cannot get any better.

Although @modeckimellett makes a good point about "not learning a second language to manipulate the first one", I don't think this is relevant here. For this to work, you must have a way to differentiate requests for data and requests for templates. The way I explored it with pure HTML was to make a request for each piece of data (that's too many) or one request for an already filled template (which seems counterproductive). There is no way to structure data in HTML, you MUST use some other language, like XML or JSON-LD. XML seems to be a natural partner for HTML. And if the solution already exists to extend XML to HTML, and the interface to apply it in an HTML document is already defined,  why not incorporate it within HTML?

@maherbo , you raised the valid concerns on the templating capabilities. Historically XSLT been banned from progress as a part of HTML stack. The primary reason is "hard" to develop in the syntax and lack of DX in the browser. 

The syntax of TEMPLATE with slots been accepted instead as primitive but DX-friendly. Due to semantic/programming language limitations that approach also failed. 

What could be a better and sustainable solution? IMO the fusion of slots for simple data replacement and XSLT for template functionality. [custom-element](https://github.com/EPA-WG/custom-element) README has details on proposed syntax and demo for XSLT-based declarative custom component. DX definitely could be improved, browser plugin should come along with polyfill. Modular development guideline should be part of the effort.

@jakearchibald yes, reading this on a web browser of which there are about 2 independent implementations in the world because HTML5 is such a complex monolithic spec ("living standard" is an oxymoron, by the way).

Where XML grammar was defined declaratively using EBNF, HTML5 uses imperative parser algorithms. This is a step back, as explained by this [HTML5 is the New Flash](https://homepages.cwi.nl/~steven/Talks/2016/05-05-html5/) presentation.

The barrier to writing an up-to-date, compliant HTML5 parser is much higher now, and pretty much out of reach of individual developers and small organizations. That was never the case with XML.

> @sasha-firsov-bs [SaxonJS](https://www.saxonica.com/saxon-js/index.xml) is exactly what you're talking about: a client-side XSLT 3.0 engine with support for DOM manipulation and event handling.

@namedgraph , SaxonJS is good as part of polyfill, for browser vendor the native C implementation is more suitable. Free OSS [libxslt](http://xmlsoft.org/xslt/xslt.html) or commercial choice from [Saxonica](https://www.saxonica.com/saxon-c/index.xml), Altova, etc. As XSLT is a standard, vendors would choose which is more performant, supported, or costly :)

As stated, XSLT engine alone is not sufficient, there is a need for data layer access update and syntax sugar adjustments.

@bahrus , the ability to interrupt/inject the content via pre- and post- load events or another methods are not relevant to `html include` alone and can be skipped in this thread.

The pattern of load transformation pipeline is essential for evolutionary architecture approach on resources and module loading. Applicable as for html modules as for css, JS and all kind of dependencies. It is necessary for co-existing of different API/implementation versions. It is essential for adoption of new proposals by polyfill substitution by native code and fallback for legacy polyfills by browser as a platform itself.

@here, please thumb up if want to participate in transformation pipeline discussion. The proposal is baking.

To anyone interested, the latest discussion stirred up some ideas to play with and I develop the following solution:

[https://github.com/maherbo/include-HTML-document-fragment](https://github.com/maherbo/include-HTML-document-fragment)

Although I have not tested the concept extensively, I'm quite satisfied with the results:

- Uses methods already implemented and optimized by the user agents, each in their own way;
- Degrades gracefully for user agents that do not have those methods (even ones not using *javascript*; although this shouldn't be a problem once such a method is implemented in the specs);
- Simply defines a few new attributes to an already defined element, i.e. the anchor element `<a>`.

@jakearchibald , html include is a particular case for DCE( declarative custom element ) - a proposal in baking for no-js web components development.

The hydration is a pattern which is used by most of scalable apps. Virtual scrolling and server side rendering are the particular cases for that. 

All of mentioned are under heavy discussions in w3c. I bet you familiar as with concepts as with discussions existence. Taking your comment as sarcasm than.

> It would just ignore all stylesheets and scripts in the fragment.

@bburns Wouldn't it be easier to simply run existing HTML parser logic on the included HTML without making new logic that clips out styles and scripts?

Plus, if styles/scripts were stripped, the include would not be the same as writing HTML inline, it destroys the ability to take inline HTML and move it into a separate file without breaking the moved HTML code. The goal is to simply put HTML in a separate file and expect it works the same.
